{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adetayo","familyName":"Adebimpe","name":"Adebimpe, Adetayo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helmut","familyName":"Neukirchen","name":"Neukirchen, Helmut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Welsh","name":"Welsh, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SBASH: a Framework for Designing and Evaluating RAG vs. Prompt-Tuned LLM Honeypots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"K.6.5; D.4.6; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:41:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T00:45:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/fllm67465.2025.11391242","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Honeypots are decoy systems used for gathering valuable threat intelligence or diverting attackers away from production systems. Maximising attacker engagement is essential to their utility. However research has highlighted that context-awareness, such as the ability to respond to new attack types, systems and attacker agents, is necessary to increase engagement. Large Language Models (LLMs) have been shown as one approach to increase context awareness but suffer from several challenges including accuracy and timeliness of response time, high operational costs and data-protection issues due to cloud deployment. We propose the System-Based Attention Shell Honeypot (SBASH) framework which manages data-protection issues through the use of lightweight local LLMs. We investigate the use of Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) supported LLMs and non-RAG LLMs for Linux shell commands and evaluate them using several different metrics such as response time differences, realism from human testers, and similarity to a real system calculated with Levenshtein distance, SBert, and BertScore. We show that RAG improves accuracy for untuned models while models that have been tuned via a system prompt that tells the LLM to respond like a Linux system achieve without RAG a similar accuracy as untuned with RAG, while having a slightly lower latency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"to be published in: The 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM2025), IEEE, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:13Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-04T03:29:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21236","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21236","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21236","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Bühler","name":"Bühler, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Biagiola","name":"Biagiola, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Di Grazia","name":"Di Grazia, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guido","familyName":"Salvaneschi","name":"Salvaneschi, Guido","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentBound: Securing Execution Boundaries of AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:10:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:33:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T13:11:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:36:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-24T09:59:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-27T00:32:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3808103","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have evolved into AI agents that interact with external tools and environments to perform complex tasks. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) has become the de facto standard for connecting agents with such resources, but security has lagged behind: thousands of MCP servers execute with unrestricted access to host systems, creating a broad attack surface. In this paper, we introduce AgentBound, the first access control framework for MCP servers. AgentBound combines a declarative policy mechanism, inspired by the Android permission model, with a policy enforcement engine that contains malicious behavior without requiring MCP server modifications. We build a dataset containing the 296 most popular MCP servers, and show that access control policies can be generated automatically from source code with 80.9% accuracy. We also show that AgentBound blocks the majority of security threats in several malicious MCP servers, and that the policy enforcement engine introduces negligible overhead. Our contributions provide developers and project managers with a foundation for securing MCP servers while maintaining productivity, enabling researchers and tool builders to explore new directions for declarative access control and MCP security."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21236","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-27T01:50:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21652","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21652","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21652","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Bragg","name":"Bragg, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike","familyName":"D'Arcy","name":"D'Arcy, Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishant","familyName":"Balepur","name":"Balepur, Nishant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Bareket","name":"Bareket, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhavana","familyName":"Dalvi","name":"Dalvi, Bhavana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Feldman","name":"Feldman, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dany","familyName":"Haddad","name":"Haddad, Dany","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jena D.","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Jena D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Jansen","name":"Jansen, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varsha","familyName":"Kishore","name":"Kishore, Varsha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bodhisattwa Prasad","familyName":"Majumder","name":"Majumder, Bodhisattwa Prasad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aakanksha","familyName":"Naik","name":"Naik, Aakanksha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sigal","familyName":"Rahamimov","name":"Rahamimov, Sigal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Richardson","name":"Richardson, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amanpreet","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Amanpreet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshit","familyName":"Surana","name":"Surana, Harshit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aryeh","familyName":"Tiktinsky","name":"Tiktinsky, Aryeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rosni","familyName":"Vasu","name":"Vasu, Rosni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guy","familyName":"Wiener","name":"Wiener, Guy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chloe","familyName":"Anastasiades","name":"Anastasiades, Chloe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Candra","name":"Candra, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Dunkelberger","name":"Dunkelberger, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Emery","name":"Emery, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rob","familyName":"Evans","name":"Evans, Rob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malachi","familyName":"Hamada","name":"Hamada, Malachi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Regan","familyName":"Huff","name":"Huff, Regan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rodney","familyName":"Kinney","name":"Kinney, Rodney","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matt","familyName":"Latzke","name":"Latzke, Matt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaron","familyName":"Lochner","name":"Lochner, Jaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruben","familyName":"Lozano-Aguilera","name":"Lozano-Aguilera, Ruben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cecile","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Cecile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Smita","familyName":"Rao","name":"Rao, Smita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amber","familyName":"Tanaka","name":"Tanaka, Amber","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brooke","familyName":"Vlahos","name":"Vlahos, Brooke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Clark","name":"Clark, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doug","familyName":"Downey","name":"Downey, Doug","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoav","familyName":"Goldberg","name":"Goldberg, Yoav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish","familyName":"Sabharwal","name":"Sabharwal, Ashish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel S.","familyName":"Weld","name":"Weld, Daniel S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AstaBench: Rigorous Benchmarking of AI Agents with a Scientific Research Suite"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:10:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:02:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T23:10:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:14:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents hold the potential to revolutionize scientific productivity by automating literature reviews, replicating experiments, analyzing data, and even proposing new directions of inquiry; indeed, there are now many such agents, ranging from general-purpose \"deep research\" systems to specialized science-specific agents, such as AI Scientist and AIGS. Rigorous evaluation of these agents is critical for progress. Yet existing benchmarks fall short on several fronts: they often (1) lack reproducible agent tools necessary for a controlled comparison of core agentic capabilities; (2) do not account for confounding variables such as model cost and tool access; (3) do not provide standardized interfaces for quick agent prototyping and evaluation; (4) fail to provide holistic, product-informed measures of real-world use cases such as science research; and (5) lack comprehensive baseline agents necessary to identify true advances. In response, we define principles and tooling for more rigorously benchmarking agents. Using these, we present AstaBench, a suite that provides a holistic measure of agentic ability to perform scientific research, comprising 2400+ problems spanning the entire scientific discovery process and multiple scientific domains, and including many problems inspired by actual user requests to deployed Asta agents. Our suite comes with the first scientific research environment with production-grade search tools that enable controlled, reproducible evaluation, better accounting for confounders. Alongside, we provide a comprehensive suite of nine science-optimized classes of Asta agents and numerous baselines. Our extensive evaluation of 57 agents across 22 agent classes reveals several interesting findings, most importantly that despite meaningful progress on certain individual aspects, AI remains far from solving the challenge of science research assistance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21652","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:36Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:23:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21413","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21413","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21413","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyedmoein","familyName":"Mohsenimofidi","name":"Mohsenimofidi, Seyedmoein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Galster","name":"Galster, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Treude","name":"Treude, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Baltes","name":"Baltes, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Context Engineering for AI Agents in Open-Source Software"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:55:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:48:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T08:53:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:30:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T22:01:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:14:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-05T19:10:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:02:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"GenAI-based coding assistants have disrupted software development. The next generation of these tools is agent-based, operating with more autonomy and potentially without human oversight. Like human developers, AI agents require contextual information to develop solutions that are in line with the standards, policies, and workflows of the software projects they operate in. Vendors of popular agentic tools (e.g., Claude Code) recommend maintaining version-controlled Markdown files that describe aspects such as the project structure, code style, or building and testing. The content of these files is then automatically added to each prompt. Recently, AGENTS$.$md has emerged as a potential standard that consolidates existing tool-specific formats. However, little is known about whether and how developers adopt this format. Therefore, in this paper, we present the results of a preliminary study investigating the adoption of AI context files in 466 open-source software projects. We analyze the information that developers provide in AGENTS$.$md files, how they present that information, and how the files evolve over time. Our findings indicate that there is no established content structure yet and that there is a lot of variation in terms of how context is provided (descriptive, prescriptive, prohibitive, explanatory, conditional). Our commit-level analysis provides first insights into the evolution of the provided context. AI context files provide a unique opportunity to study real-world context engineering. In particular, we see great potential in studying which structural or presentational modifications can positively affect the quality of the generated content."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 1 figure, 2 tables, 23rd IEEE/ACM International Conference on Mining Software Repositories (MSR 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21413","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:08Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-09T03:02:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21618","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21618","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21618","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxiang","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Wenxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanting","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Guanting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yinuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji-Rong","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Ji-Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepAgent: A General Reasoning Agent with Scalable Toolsets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:24:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-04T08:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T01:33:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T14:08:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:49:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large reasoning models have demonstrated strong problem-solving abilities, yet real-world tasks often require external tools and long-horizon interactions. Existing agent frameworks typically follow predefined workflows, which limit autonomous and global task completion. In this paper, we introduce DeepAgent, an end-to-end deep reasoning agent that performs autonomous thinking, tool discovery, and action execution within a single, coherent reasoning process. To manage long-horizon interactions, we introduce an autonomous memory folding mechanism that compresses past interactions into structured episodic, working, and tool memories, reducing error accumulation while preserving critical information. To teach general-purpose tool use efficiently and stably, we develop an end-to-end reinforcement learning strategy, namely ToolPO, that leverages LLM-simulated APIs and applies tool-call advantage attribution to assign fine-grained credit to the tool invocation tokens. Extensive experiments on eight benchmarks, including general tool-use tasks (ToolBench, API-Bank, TMDB, Spotify, ToolHop) and downstream applications (ALFWorld, WebShop, GAIA, HLE), demonstrate that DeepAgent consistently outperforms baselines across both labeled-tool and open-set tool retrieval scenarios. The code and demo are available at https://github.com/RUC-NLPIR/DeepAgent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by WWW 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21618","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:49Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:22:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21144","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21144","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21144","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lance","familyName":"Fiondella","name":"Fiondella, Lance","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NeuroGenPoisoning: Neuron-Guided Attacks on Retrieval-Augmented Generation of LLM via Genetic Optimization of External Knowledge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:30:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:06:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T21:36:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:56:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) empowers Large Language Models (LLMs) to dynamically integrate external knowledge during inference, improving their factual accuracy and adaptability. However, adversaries can inject poisoned external knowledge to override the model's internal memory. While existing attacks iteratively manipulate retrieval content or prompt structure of RAG, they largely ignore the model's internal representation dynamics and neuron-level sensitivities. The underlying mechanism of RAG poisoning has not been fully studied and the effect of knowledge conflict with strong parametric knowledge in RAG is not considered. In this work, we propose NeuroGenPoisoning, a novel attack framework that generates adversarial external knowledge in RAG guided by LLM internal neuron attribution and genetic optimization. Our method first identifies a set of Poison-Responsive Neurons whose activation strongly correlates with contextual poisoning knowledge. We then employ a genetic algorithm to evolve adversarial passages that maximally activate these neurons. Crucially, our framework enables massive-scale generation of effective poisoned RAG knowledge by identifying and reusing promising but initially unsuccessful external knowledge variants via observed attribution signals. At the same time, Poison-Responsive Neurons guided poisoning can effectively resolves knowledge conflict. Experimental results across models and datasets demonstrate consistently achieving high Population Overwrite Success Rate (POSR) of over 90% while preserving fluency. Empirical evidence shows that our method effectively resolves knowledge conflict."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21144","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:27:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21341","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21341","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21341","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lufan","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Lufan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Magellan: Guided MCTS for Latent Space Exploration and Novelty Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:09:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:42:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T02:33:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:19:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) often struggle with generating truly innovative ideas, typically defaulting to high-probability, familiar concepts within their training data's \"gravity wells.\" While advanced search-based methods like Tree of Thoughts (ToT) attempt to mitigate this, they are fundamentally limited by their reliance on unprincipled, inconsistent self-evaluation heuristics to guide exploration. To address this gap, we introduce \\textbf{Magellan}, a novel framework that reframes creative generation as a principled, guided exploration of an LLM's latent conceptual space. At its core, Magellan employs Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) governed by a hierarchical guidance system. For long-range direction, a \"semantic compass\" vector, formulated via orthogonal projection, steers the search towards relevant novelty. For local, step-by-step decisions, a landscape-aware value function replaces flawed self-evaluation with an explicit reward structure that balances intrinsic coherence, extrinsic novelty, and narrative progress. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Magellan significantly outperforms strong baselines, including ReAct and ToT, in generating scientific ideas with superior plausibility and innovation. Our work shows that for creative discovery, a principled, guided search is more effective than unconstrained agency, paving the way for LLMs to become more capable partners in innovation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to 1st Open Conference on AI Agents for Science (agents4science 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21341","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:27Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:17:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21117","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21117","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21117","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agostino","familyName":"Capponi","name":"Capponi, Agostino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfio","familyName":"Gliozzo","name":"Gliozzo, Alfio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunghyun","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Chunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junkyu","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Junkyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DAO-AI: Evaluating Collective Decision-Making through Agentic AI in Decentralized Governance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:13:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:36:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:15:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a first empirical study of agentic AI as autonomous decision-makers in decentralized governance. Using more than 3K proposals from major protocols, we build an agentic AI voter that interprets proposal contexts, retrieves historical deliberation data, and independently determines its voting position. The agent operates within a realistic financial simulation environment grounded in verifiable blockchain data, implemented through a modular composable program (MCP) workflow that defines data flow and tool usage via Agentics framework. We evaluate how closely the agent's decisions align with the human and token-weighted outcomes, uncovering strong alignments measured by carefully designed evaluation metrics. Our findings demonstrate that agentic AI can augment collective decision-making by producing interpretable, auditable, and empirically grounded signals in realistic DAO governance settings. The study contributes to the design of explainable and economically rigorous AI agents for decentralized financial systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 2 Figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21117","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:08Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:06:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21656","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21656","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21656","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marta Contreiras","familyName":"Silva","name":"Silva, Marta Contreiras","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Faria","name":"Faria, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catia","familyName":"Pesquita","name":"Pesquita, Catia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CMOMgen: Complex Multi-Ontology Alignment via Pattern-Guided In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:12:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:02:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Constructing comprehensive knowledge graphs requires the use of multiple ontologies in order to fully contextualize data into a domain. Ontology matching finds equivalences between concepts interconnecting ontologies and creating a cohesive semantic layer. While the simple pairwise state of the art is well established, simple equivalence mappings cannot provide full semantic integration of related but disjoint ontologies. Complex multi-ontology matching (CMOM) aligns one source entity to composite logical expressions of multiple target entities, establishing more nuanced equivalences and provenance along the ontological hierarchy.\n We present CMOMgen, the first end-to-end CMOM strategy that generates complete and semantically sound mappings, without establishing any restrictions on the number of target ontologies or entities. Retrieval-Augmented Generation selects relevant classes to compose the mapping and filters matching reference mappings to serve as examples, enhancing In-Context Learning. The strategy was evaluated in three biomedical tasks with partial reference alignments. CMOMgen outperforms baselines in class selection, demonstrating the impact of having a dedicated strategy. Our strategy also achieves a minimum of 63% in F1-score, outperforming all baselines and ablated versions in two out of three tasks and placing second in the third. Furthermore, a manual evaluation of non-reference mappings showed that 46% of the mappings achieve the maximum score, further substantiating its ability to construct semantically sound mappings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21656","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:42Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:59:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21538","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21538","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21538","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Likun","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Likun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan-Wei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kuan-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joy","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Joy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InterpDetect: Interpretable Signals for Detecting Hallucinations in Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:02:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:56:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) integrates external knowledge to mitigate hallucinations, yet models often generate outputs inconsistent with retrieved content. Accurate hallucination detection requires disentangling the contributions of external context and parametric knowledge, which prior methods typically conflate. We investigate the mechanisms underlying RAG hallucinations and find they arise when later-layer FFN modules disproportionately inject parametric knowledge into the residual stream. To address this, we explore a mechanistic detection approach based on external context scores and parametric knowledge scores. Using Qwen3-0.6b, we compute these scores across layers and attention heads and train regression-based classifiers to predict hallucinations. Our method is evaluated against state-of-the-art LLMs (GPT-5, GPT-4.1) and detection baselines (RAGAS, TruLens, RefChecker). Furthermore, classifiers trained on Qwen3-0.6b signals generalize to GPT-4.1-mini responses, demonstrating the potential of proxy-model evaluation. Our results highlight mechanistic signals as efficient, generalizable predictors for hallucination detection in RAG systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21538","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21440","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21440","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21440","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Trappolini","name":"Trappolini, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florin","familyName":"Cuconasu","name":"Cuconasu, Florin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Filice","name":"Filice, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoelle","familyName":"Maarek","name":"Maarek, Yoelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrizio","familyName":"Silvestri","name":"Silvestri, Fabrizio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Redefining Retrieval Evaluation in the Era of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:17:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:49:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Traditional Information Retrieval (IR) metrics, such as nDCG, MAP, and MRR, assume that human users sequentially examine documents with diminishing attention to lower ranks. This assumption breaks down in Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) systems, where search results are consumed by Large Language Models (LLMs), which, unlike humans, process all retrieved documents as a whole rather than sequentially. Additionally, traditional IR metrics do not account for related but irrelevant documents that actively degrade generation quality, rather than merely being ignored. Due to these two major misalignments, namely human vs. machine position discount and human relevance vs. machine utility, classical IR metrics do not accurately predict RAG performance. We introduce a utility-based annotation schema that quantifies both the positive contribution of relevant passages and the negative impact of distracting ones. Building on this foundation, we propose UDCG (Utility and Distraction-aware Cumulative Gain), a metric using an LLM-oriented positional discount to directly optimize the correlation with the end-to-end answer accuracy. Experiments on five datasets and six LLMs demonstrate that UDCG improves correlation by up to 36% compared to traditional metrics. Our work provides a critical step toward aligning IR evaluation with LLM consumers and enables more reliable assessment of RAG components"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21440","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21228","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21228","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21228","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huizi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Huizi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenkong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiran","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyue","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Wenyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjia","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Wenjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lexuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Bingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming-Li","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ming-Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Themistocles L.","familyName":"Assimes","name":"Assimes, Themistocles L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qingyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhou","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Lizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DispatchMAS: Fusing taxonomy and artificial intelligence agents for emergency medical services"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 92C50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:01:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:32:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Objective: Emergency medical dispatch (EMD) is a high-stakes process challenged by caller distress, ambiguity, and cognitive load. Large Language Models (LLMs) and Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) offer opportunities to augment dispatchers. This study aimed to develop and evaluate a taxonomy-grounded, LLM-powered multi-agent system for simulating realistic EMD scenarios. Methods: We constructed a clinical taxonomy (32 chief complaints, 6 caller identities from MIMIC-III) and a six-phase call protocol. Using this framework, we developed an AutoGen-based MAS with Caller and Dispatcher Agents. The system grounds interactions in a fact commons to ensure clinical plausibility and mitigate misinformation. We used a hybrid evaluation framework: four physicians assessed 100 simulated cases for \"Guidance Efficacy\" and \"Dispatch Effectiveness,\" supplemented by automated linguistic analysis (sentiment, readability, politeness). Results: Human evaluation, with substantial inter-rater agreement (Gwe's AC1 \u0026gt; 0.70), confirmed the system's high performance. It demonstrated excellent Dispatch Effectiveness (e.g., 94 % contacting the correct potential other agents) and Guidance Efficacy (advice provided in 91 % of cases), both rated highly by physicians. Algorithmic metrics corroborated these findings, indicating a predominantly neutral affective profile (73.7 % neutral sentiment; 90.4 % neutral emotion), high readability (Flesch 80.9), and a consistently polite style (60.0 % polite; 0 % impolite). Conclusion: Our taxonomy-grounded MAS simulates diverse, clinically plausible dispatch scenarios with high fidelity. Findings support its use for dispatcher training, protocol evaluation, and as a foundation for real-time decision support. This work outlines a pathway for safely integrating advanced AI agents into emergency response workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 7 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21228","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chujun","familyName":"He","name":"He, Chujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhonghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangguo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiangguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kewei","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Kewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchical AI Multi-Agent Fundamental Investing: Evidence from China's A-Share Market"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Portfolio Management (q-fin.PM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:38:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:24:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a multi-agent, AI-driven framework for fundamental investing that integrates macro indicators, industry-level and firm-specific information to construct optimized equity portfolios. The architecture comprises: (i) a Macro agent that dynamically screens and weights sectors based on evolving economic indicators and industry performance; (ii) four firm-level agents -- Fundamental, Technical, Report, and News -- that conduct in-depth analyses of individual firms to ensure both breadth and depth of coverage; (iii) a Portfolio agent that uses reinforcement learning to combine the agent outputs into a unified policy to generate the trading strategy; and (iv) a Risk Control agent that adjusts portfolio positions in response to market volatility. We evaluate the system on the constituents by the CSI 300 Index of China's A-share market and find that it consistently outperforms standard benchmarks and a state-of-the-art multi-agent trading system on risk-adjusted returns and drawdown control. Our core contribution is a hierarchical multi-agent design that links top-down macro screening with bottom-up fundamental analysis, offering a robust and extensible approach to factor-based portfolio construction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:51Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbo","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jinbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tenghui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tenghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xumin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanjia","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yuanjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hudan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Hudan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zishao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengli","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Shengli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong In","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dong In","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedAlign: A Synergistic Framework of Multimodal Preference Optimization and Federated Meta-Cognitive Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:11:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:17:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, large models have shown significant potential for smart healthcare. However, the deployment of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) for clinical services is currently hindered by three critical challenges: a tendency to hallucinate answers not grounded in visual evidence, the inefficiency of fixed-depth reasoning, and the difficulty of multi-institutional collaboration. To address these challenges, in this paper, we develop MedAlign, a novel framework to ensure visually accurate LVLM responses for Medical Visual Question Answering (Med-VQA). Specifically, we first propose a multimodal Direct Preference Optimization (mDPO) objective to explicitly align preference learning with visual context. We then design a Retrieval-Aware Mixture-of-Experts (RA-MoE) architecture that utilizes image and text similarity to route queries to a specialized and context-augmented LVLM (i.e., an expert), thereby mitigating hallucinations in LVLMs. To achieve adaptive reasoning and facilitate multi-institutional collaboration, we propose a federated governance mechanism, where the selected expert, fine-tuned on clinical datasets based on mDPO, locally performs iterative Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning via the local meta-cognitive uncertainty estimator. Extensive experiments on three representative Med-VQA datasets demonstrate that MedAlign achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming strong retrieval-augmented baselines by up to $11.85\\%$ in F1-score, and simultaneously reducing the average reasoning length by $51.60\\%$ compared with fixed-depth CoT approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Christian","name":"Christian, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Adamlu","name":"Adamlu, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Adrian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derwin","familyName":"Suhartono","name":"Suhartono, Derwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Language Gaps with Adaptive RAG: Improving Indonesian Language Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T00:50:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:14:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Question Answering (QA) has seen significant improvements with the advancement of machine learning models, further studies enhanced this question answering system by retrieving external information, called Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to produce more accurate and informative answers. However, these state-of-the-art-performance is predominantly in English language. To address this gap we made an effort of bridging language gaps by incorporating Adaptive RAG system to Indonesian language. Adaptive RAG system integrates a classifier whose task is to distinguish the question complexity, which in turn determines the strategy for answering the question. To overcome the limited availability of Indonesian language dataset, our study employs machine translation as data augmentation approach. Experiments show reliable question complexity classifier; however, we observed significant inconsistencies in multi-retrieval answering strategy which negatively impacted the overall evaluation when this strategy was applied. These findings highlight both the promise and challenges of question answering in low-resource language suggesting directions for future improvement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 7 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20878","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20878","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danying","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Danying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhua","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixue","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yixue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixing","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Weixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HA-RAG: Hotness-Aware RAG Acceleration via Mixed Precision and Data Placement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.4; E.4; I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T12:28:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) improves model output accuracy by leveraging external knowledge bases, serving as an effective solution to address hallucination issues and knowledge-update delays in Large Language Models (LLMs). However, the introduction of external knowledge bases presents RAG with challenges in long-context processing, significantly increasing memory consumption and inference latency. Existing research accelerates inference by precomputing Key and Value (KV) of the knowledge base and loading them on-demand during inference. Based on the access frequency of different KV chunks within the external knowledge base, this paper proposes a hotness-aware RAG (HA-RAG) inference optimization system. First, leveraging the numerical distribution of KV chunks, we introduce a hotness-aware mixed-precision compressing and loading method to reduce disk I/O and memory access overhead. Second, we design a hotness-aware data placement strategy that prioritizes storing frequently accessed KV chunks in high-speed memory to improve data access efficiency. Experimental results demonstrate that, compared with TurboRAG, the proposed HA-RAG achieves an average speedup of 2.10x and maximum speedup of 10.49x in Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) with negligible accuracy loss."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages,16 figures,2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":16,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-26T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-27T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+agent%22+OR+%22language+agents%22+OR+%22AI+agent%22+OR+%22AI+agents%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22retrieval+augmented%22+OR+%22retrieval-augmented%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+agent%22+OR+%22language+agents%22+OR+%22AI+agent%22+OR+%22AI+agents%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22retrieval+augmented%22+OR+%22retrieval-augmented%22%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100"}}